Feature: Connectors read what an upstream allows now from its own answers
  spec/PLAN-OPENAPI-GATEWAY.md, Connectors. Many APIs that publish only
  OpenAPI still say, per resource, what can be done to it now: a list of
  action names, a map of booleans, an array of links, or OData's advertised
  actions. Others say nothing, and someone who knows the API writes the
  rules down.

  A connector is one API's folder: a connector.yaml naming its profile and
  listing its readers in order, and one file per reader. Each reader says
  what answers it can read (by the body, a header, or the path the answer
  came from), and the first that can, reads it. A reader that matches everything is the API's default. Standards need
  no connector: HEART's affordance link, and OData's advertised actions.

  The upstream here is a small service of doors. A door is OPEN, CLOSED or
  LOCKED; a closed door can be opened or locked, an open one closed, and a
  locked one unlocked. Some doors are vault doors.

  Scenario Outline: An API's default reader reads its <signal>, and the gateway offers what it allows
    Given the door upstream signals by <signal>, read by the connector "<connector>"
    And door 1 is "CLOSED"
    When a client gets door 1 through the gateway
    Then the gateway offers "doors:open, doors:lock"
    And the door's state has no member "<hidden>"

    Examples:
      | signal           | connector      | hidden         |
      | a names list     | doors-names    | allowedActions |
      | a boolean map    | doors-booleans | capabilities   |
      | a link array     | doors-links    | links          |
      | OData actions    | doors-odata    | #Doors.open    |
      | a state table    | doors-table    | allowedActions |
      | a JSONata file   | doors-jsonata  | perms          |

  Scenario: After an act, the gateway offers what the door's new answer allows
    Given the door upstream signals by a names list, read by the connector "doors-names"
    And door 1 is "CLOSED"
    When a client gets door 1 through the gateway
    And the client sends "doors:open" with the ETag it holds
    Then the response status is 200
    And the gateway offers "doors:close"

  Scenario: A description that names its HEART relations is followed, so a renamed relation reads as its server names it
    A description generated by a HEART server says which relation each
    operation is (x-heart-relation). The gateway names the act by it, not
    by the operation's id, so a relation renamed while its operation keeps
    its id reads through the gateway exactly as it does natively.
    Given the door upstream signals by a names list, read by the connector "doors-names"
    And the door upstream's description names the operation "open" as the relation "doors:swing-open"
    And door 1 is "CLOSED"
    When a client gets door 1 through the gateway
    Then the gateway offers "doors:swing-open, doors:lock"

  Scenario: A state table says why an act is absent
    Given the door upstream signals by a state table, read by the connector "doors-table"
    And door 1 is "LOCKED"
    When a client gets door 1 through the gateway
    Then the gateway offers "doors:unlock"
    And the gateway says "doors:open" is absent because "The door is locked; unlock it first."

  Scenario Outline: A reader that matches by <what> reads the answers it recognizes, and the default reads the rest
    The vault doors' reader is listed first; the API's default reader last.
    Given the door upstream signals by a names list, read by the connector "<connector>"
    And door 1 is "LOCKED"
    And door 2 is a vault door, "LOCKED"
    When a client gets door 2 through the gateway
    Then the gateway says "doors:open" is absent because "A vault door opens only with two keys."
    When a client gets door 1 through the gateway
    Then the gateway says "doors:open" is absent because "The upstream doesn't allow it in the resource's current state."

    Examples:
      | what                  | connector             |
      | a member of the body  | doors-vault-by-body   |
      | the answer's media type | doors-vault-by-header |

  Scenario: A reader can match the path the answer came from, by the description's path template
    Given the door upstream signals by a names list, read by the connector "doors-by-path"
    And door 1 is "CLOSED"
    When a client gets door 1 through the gateway
    Then the gateway offers "doors:open, doors:lock"

  Scenario: With no connector, the gateway reads OData's advertised actions, a standard, by itself
    Given the door upstream signals by OData actions, with no connector
    And door 1 is "CLOSED"
    When a client gets door 1 through the gateway
    Then the gateway offers "doors:open, doors:lock"
    And the door's state has no member "#Doors.open"

  Scenario Outline: A connector with a mistake stops the gateway at the start: <mistake>
    Given the door upstream signals by a names list, read by the connector "<connector>"
    When the gateway starts
    Then the gateway refuses to start, saying "<says>"

    Examples:
      | mistake                       | connector      | says                                                                         |
      | an operation the API lacks    | doors-broken   | doors-broken/door.reader.yaml: the upstream's description has no operation fly |
      | a reader the list leaves out  | doors-unlisted | doors-unlisted/vault.reader.yaml: not listed under readers in connector.yaml |
      | a path the API doesn't serve  | doors-nowhere  | doors-nowhere/door.reader.yaml: the upstream's description serves no /windows/{id} |
